    <section class="content rob-content" aria-labelledby="howHeading">
      <h2 id="howHeading">How this robots.txt tool works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Build a file.</strong> Add a user-agent group, then add Allow or Disallow rules. A group is one crawler name followed by the paths it may or may not visit.</li>
        <li><strong>Use a preset.</strong> Presets replace the current builder with a known-good starting point. Adjust the rules after applying one.</li>
        <li><strong>Generate.</strong> The output panel shows the final file. Copy it or download it as <code>robots.txt</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Validate a file you already have.</strong> Switch to Validate, paste the file, and click Validate. Errors and warnings appear with line numbers.</li>
        <li><strong>Test a URL.</strong> Enter a path and a user-agent. The tester answers whether that crawler may fetch that path, using the same matching rules Google and Bing apply.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What robots.txt can and cannot do</h2>
      <p>robots.txt tells crawlers which paths they may request. It does not prevent a page from appearing in search results. A blocked page can still be indexed if other sites link to it, because the crawler never sees the page but the URL is known. To keep a page out of search, use a <code>noindex</code> meta tag or an <code>X-Robots-Tag</code> header instead.</p>
      <p>robots.txt is advisory. Well-behaved crawlers follow it. Malicious scrapers, some security scanners and a few small search engines ignore it. It is not a security mechanism and should never be used to protect private data.</p>

      <h2>How matching works</h2>
      <p>When a crawler reads a robots.txt file, it does four things in order:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Finds its own group.</strong> A crawler first looks for a group whose <code>User-agent</code> line matches its name exactly. If none exists, it falls back to the <code>User-agent: *</code> group.</li>
        <li><strong>Collects every Allow and Disallow rule</strong> from the matched group. If multiple groups exist for the same agent, their rules are combined.</li>
        <li><strong>Finds the longest matching path.</strong> A rule whose path matches the start of the URL is a candidate. The rule with the longest path wins.</li>
        <li><strong>Breaks ties in favour of Allow.</strong> If an Allow and a Disallow rule have paths of equal length, the Allow rule wins. If no rule matches, the URL is allowed.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="rob-table-wrap">
        <table class="rob-table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Examples of matching precedence</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Rules</th>
              <th scope="col">Requested path</th>
              <th scope="col">Result</th>
              <th scope="col">Reason</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>Disallow: /admin/</code><br><code>Allow: /admin/public/</code></td>
              <td><code>/admin/public/page</code></td>
              <td>Allowed</td>
              <td>The Allow path is longer and matches</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>Disallow: /page</code><br><code>Allow: /page-</code></td>
              <td><code>/page-two</code></td>
              <td>Allowed</td>
              <td>The Allow path is longer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>Disallow: /private</code></td>
              <td><code>/public</code></td>
              <td>Allowed</td>
              <td>No rule matches, so the default is allowed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>Disallow: /</code></td>
              <td><code>/anything</code></td>
              <td>Blocked</td>
              <td>Every path matches the root rule</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Wildcards</h2>
      <p>Google and Bing support two wildcards in Allow and Disallow paths:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>*</code> matches any sequence of characters, including an empty sequence. <code>Disallow: /*.pdf$</code> blocks every PDF file anywhere on the site.</li>
        <li><code>$</code> anchors the match to the end of the URL. Without it, a path is treated as a prefix, so <code>Disallow: /page</code> also matches <code>/page-two</code> and <code>/page/three</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Paths are case-sensitive. <code>/File.asp</code> and <code>/file.asp</code> are different rules. The <code>User-agent</code> name is case-insensitive, so <code>Googlebot</code> and <code>googlebot</code> behave the same way.</p>

      <h2>Common mistakes this tool warns about</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Blocking CSS and JavaScript.</strong> Google renders pages with a browser-like engine. If robots.txt blocks <code>/css/</code> or <code>/js/</code>, Google cannot see the page as a user would, which can hurt indexing. The validator warns when a rule blocks these paths.</li>
        <li><strong>Using <code>Disallow: /</code> on a live site.</strong> This blocks every crawler from every page. It is only appropriate on a staging or development domain. The validator flags it when the default <code>*</code> group blocks the root.</li>
        <li><strong>Rules before a User-agent line.</strong> A rule that appears before any <code>User-agent</code> line has no group to belong to and is ignored.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing colon.</strong> Every directive must be written as <code>Name: value</code>. A line without a colon is ignored by most crawlers.</li>
        <li><strong>Relative sitemap URLs.</strong> The <code>Sitemap</code> directive needs a full URL, including <code>https://</code>. <code>Sitemap: /sitemap.xml</code> is not valid.</li>
        <li><strong>Crawl-delay on Google.</strong> Google does not support <code>Crawl-delay</code> and ignores it. Bing reads it. If you need to slow Google down, use Search Console instead.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>AI crawler controls</h2>
      <p>AI companies run separate crawlers for training data and for search. Blocking one does not block the other. The AI preset adds groups for the most common ones:</p>

      <div class="rob-table-wrap">
        <table class="rob-table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">AI crawlers and their purpose</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">User-agent</th>
              <th scope="col">Operator</th>
              <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>GPTBot</code></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>Training data collection</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ClaudeBot</code></td><td>Anthropic</td><td>Training data collection</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CCBot</code></td><td>Common Crawl</td><td>Open training datasets</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Bytespider</code></td><td>ByteDance</td><td>Training and product data</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Google-Extended</code></td><td>Google</td><td>Gemini training only. Does not affect Google Search indexing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>PerplexityBot</code></td><td>Perplexity</td><td>Search index and citation</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Blocking these agents keeps your content out of their training data. It does not remove pages that are already indexed from Google Search, and it does not prevent a human from reading the page.</p>

      <h2>Where the file goes</h2>
      <p>The file must be named <code>robots.txt</code>, saved as plain text with UTF-8 encoding, and placed at the root of the domain. For <code>https://example.com/</code>, the file lives at <code>https://example.com/robots.txt</code>. It cannot be in a subdirectory, and each subdomain needs its own file.</p>
      <p>On Cloudflare Workers with static assets, upload the file to the asset directory that serves the site root. Do not route it through a Worker unless you need dynamic content. Check that the response returns HTTP 200 with <code>Content-Type: text/plain</code>.</p>

      <h2>Limitations to know</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Robots.txt does not block indexing.</strong> Use <code>noindex</code> for that.</li>
        <li><strong>Not all crawlers obey it.</strong> Scrapers and malicious bots ignore it entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Google ignores <code>Crawl-delay</code>.</strong> The validator warns about it but does not remove it, because other crawlers read it.</li>
        <li><strong>Wildcard behaviour varies.</strong> Google and Bing support <code>*</code> and <code>$</code>. Some older crawlers treat them as literal characters. The tester follows the Google interpretation.</li>
        <li><strong>The tester is a simulation.</strong> It uses the parsed rules from this page. It does not fetch a live robots.txt from another domain.</li>
        <li><strong>Nothing is saved.</strong> Reloading the page clears the builder, the validator and the tester.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="rob-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is a robots.txt file?</summary>
          <p>A plain text file at the root of a website that tells crawlers which paths they may request. It is part of the Robots Exclusion Protocol, standardised as RFC 9309.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Does blocking a page in robots.txt remove it from Google?</summary>
          <p>No. Robots.txt controls crawling, not indexing. A blocked page can still appear in search results if other sites link to it. Use a <code>noindex</code> meta tag or header to remove a page from search.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Which crawler wins if I have both a named group and a wildcard group?</summary>
          <p>The named group. A crawler matches its exact name first and only falls back to <code>*</code> when no group names it. If several groups name the same crawler, their rules are combined.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Why does an Allow rule beat a Disallow rule?</summary>
          <p>When an Allow and a Disallow rule have paths of equal length, the Allow rule wins. When the paths differ in length, the longer rule wins regardless of type. This is the RFC 9309 precedence rule that Google and Bing follow.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I block AI training without blocking search?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Add a separate group for each AI crawler (<code>GPTBot</code>, <code>ClaudeBot</code>, <code>CCBot</code>) with <code>Disallow: /</code>, while leaving <code>User-agent: *</code> with <code>Allow: /</code>. Google Search uses Googlebot, which is a separate user agent from Google-Extended.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Why does the validator warn about blocking CSS and JavaScript?</summary>
          <p>Google renders pages with a browser-like engine. If robots.txt blocks <code>/css/</code> or <code>/js/</code>, Google cannot see the page as a user would, which can lower its assessment of the page. It is one of the most common robots.txt mistakes.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Does the URL tester fetch a live robots.txt from another site?</summary>
          <p>No. The tester only tests against the rules you have built or pasted on this page. It does not make network requests.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Is my robots.txt content uploaded anywhere?</summary>
          <p>No. The builder, validator and tester all run in your browser. Nothing is sent to a server, and nothing is stored after you close the tab.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
